  <h1 class="hidetitle">Too Many DevEx Metrics, Too Little Guidance</h1>
  <h2>DevEx Metrics Compass: making sense of developer experience measurement</h2>
  <h3>André N. Meyer, Patrick Meyer, Gail C. Murphy, and Thomas Fritz</h3>
  <p>As AI-augmented development becomes standard practice, engineering leaders face mounting pressure to demonstrate its impact. Yet most organizations are measuring AI adoption and output while the effect on developer experience (DevEx) remains largely unknown. The right metrics can close that gap, surfacing the everyday friction developers face and monitoring the real impact of initiatives such as AI rollouts. But with more than 120 metrics in circulation across frameworks, companies, and scientific literature, identifying the right, meaningful, actionable ones for a specific context has become a challenge in itself.</p>
  <p>This article introduces <a href="https://devexcompass.com/">DevEx Metrics Compass</a>, a public, open-source web app built on a structured analysis of that landscape across more than 50 engineering organizations. Alongside the tool, we share what the dataset reveals about how DevEx is measured today and where the gaps lie. Whether you are new to DevEx measurement and looking for a first meaningful set of metrics, or a seasoned practitioner assessing whether an existing collection has the right breadth and depth, Compass helps teams navigate the fragmented landscape and identify measures that actually matter for their context and goals.</p>
  <div style="border:1px solid black;padding:20px;margin:20px;">
   <p>Alex, VP of engineering at a financial technology (fintech) scale-up, is attending the monthly leadership meeting. The CEO turns to Alex: “We’ve rolled out generative AI tools across engineering this year. What has the impact been on our developers’ productivity and experience?” Alex already has some telemetry data, including AI tool adoption rates, percentage of AI-written code, throughput, and deployment frequency rates, as well as a handful of responses from a one-time developer satisfaction survey sent out a few months ago. None of it adds up to a clear answer. Alex promises the CEO to come back with a clearer picture at the next leadership meeting, but with more than 120 metrics in circulation across nine frameworks, Alex isn’t sure where to start.</p>
  </div>
  <p>This is not an unusual situation. As AI-augmented coding becomes standard practice, with 93 percent of developers now using AI tools,<sup>16</sup> engineering leaders face mounting pressure to demonstrate impact. Yet most organizations lack the measurement foundation to do so. The 2025 DevOps Research and Assessment (DORA) report shows that AI adoption tends to increase individual productivity and job satisfaction. However, it can also add cognitive load on complex tasks and invite higher output expectations without reducing demands on developers,<sup>1</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>5</sup> a tension that is impossible to detect, let alone navigate, without the right measurement in place.</p>
  <p>AI has prompted many leaders and managers to ask questions they hadn’t asked before: How is AI actually changing how my developers work, and is it for the better? Those questions land squarely in the domain of developer experience. DevEx, for short, is a field concerned not just with tools and environments, but with the lived experience of developers: the friction they encounter, the flow they lose, and the conditions that determine whether they can do their best work sustainably and productively.<sup>10</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>13</sup></p>
  <p>Frameworks such as SPACE, DORA, and DX Core 4 have helped organizations think more rigorously about the measurement of DevEx. But with an ever-growing list of frameworks and possible metrics, knowing where to start has become its own challenge. Interviews with more than 20 DevEx and engineering leaders consistently surfaced the same frustration: The problem is not a lack of metrics, but knowing which ones are relevant for their specific context and goals and how to instrument and interpret them to drive meaningful action.</p>
  <p>This article introduces the <a href="https://devexcompass.com/">DevEx Metrics Compass</a>, a public, open-source web app built on a structured analysis of 120-plus metrics drawn from scientific literature and the public practices of more than 50 engineering organizations. The article shares what the data reveals about how DevEx is measured today, where the gaps are, and how to identify suitable metrics—whether you are just getting started or looking to extend an existing DevEx measurement initiative.</p>
  <p>&nbsp;</p>
  <h3>More Metrics, More Frameworks, More Confusion: The State of DevEx Measurement</h3>
  <p>Measuring DevEx is genuinely hard.<sup>12</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>15</sup> On the surface, the challenge seems like a data problem; engineering teams generate enormous amounts of data: commits, pull requests (PRs), build times, chat messages, and AI meeting transcripts. But raw activity data tells you little about whether developers are productive, satisfied, or burning out. Answering those questions requires a different kind of measurement effort, combining automated signals from development systems with self-reported data from surveys and interviews. The right combination depends heavily on where an organization is in its measurement maturity and what it is trying to achieve; a small team running a yearly satisfaction survey to understand dev-tooling friction is in a very different position from an organization with a dedicated DevEx team, established data pipelines, and custom instrumentation across the entire development lifecycle.</p>
  <p>More than nine frameworks have emerged to help. Here is a summary of the three most prominent ones:</p>
  <p>– DORA<sup>7</sup> provides a focused set of delivery-performance metrics (i.e., deployment frequency, change lead time, mean time to recover [MTTR], change fail rate, and deployment rework rate) that has become an industry standard. Its strength is its focus on delivery performance, though it does not address DevEx more broadly, including team dynamics or the conditions that make sustainable delivery possible.</p>
  <p>– SPACE<sup>8</sup> introduced a multidimensional view of developer productivity spanning satisfaction, performance, activity, communication, and efficiency. Rather than prescribing a fixed metric set, it offers a framework from which teams derive measures suited to their own context—explicitly pushing back against single-metric thinking.</p>
  <p>– DX Core 4<sup>14</sup> is a commercial framework developed by the company DX designed to unify DORA, SPACE, and the DevEx framework<sup>13</sup> into a single actionable framework organized around speed, effectiveness, quality, and business impact. Unlike SPACE, it is prescriptive rather than navigational: The framework is shaped conceptually by team collaboration and psychological dimensions such as cognitive load and flow state, but these are not tracked as separate, explicit metrics. Its composite Developer Experience Index (DXI) is also proprietary, which can make it harder to see exactly how scores are constructed or how to reproduce comparisons across organizations.</p>
  <p>Each has its merit in specific contexts. But taken together, they have also contributed to a fragmented landscape: overlapping definitions, inconsistent operationalization, and no clear guidance on which framework, or which specific metrics within it, best fits a given context, maturity level, and goal.</p>
  <p>A growing ecosystem of developer intelligence platforms has emerged alongside these frameworks, including <a href="https://getdx.com/">DX</a>, <a href="https://linearb.io/">LinearB</a>, <a href="https://getquotient.com/">Quotient</a>, and <a href="https://www.faros.ai/">Faros</a>. Each takes a different approach to metric selection: DX pairs self-reported developer sentiment with system data from tools like GitHub and Jira, organized around its DX Core 4 framework and composite index (DXI); LinearB focuses on benchmarking and goal setting; Quotient automatically surfaces bottlenecks and recommends actions; and Faros aggregates engineering data across tools into a unified data platform, enabling custom metric definitions and analysis. These tools are valuable, but they address a different problem: tracking and monitoring a predefined set of metrics rather than helping organizations determine which metrics to track in the first place, and why.</p>
  <p>The stakes of getting this wrong are high. Measuring the wrong things doesn’t just waste effort; it can be actively harmful, creating wrong incentives, gaming, and misaligned pressure on engineering teams. As Goodhart’s law warns: “When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.”</p>
  <p>&nbsp;</p>
  <h3>Five Misconceptions About DevEx Measurement</h3>
  <p>Despite growing awareness of DevEx as a strategic concern, several persistent misconceptions continue to undermine measurement efforts in practice. Drawing on our research and conversations with DevEx and engineering leaders, the following five patterns stand out as particularly common and consequential:</p>
  <p>– <b>Having more metrics means better insight.</b> Adding metrics without a clear hypothesis or goal creates noise, not clarity. A more effective approach is to start with the problem you want to solve: Define a hypothesis about what might be causing it, then identify metrics that will help you observe whether an intervention improves or worsens the situation. The challenge is not accumulation but deliberate selection.</p>
  <p>– <b>What is easy to measure is worth measuring.</b> Automated metrics, such as number of commits, PR counts, AI token spend, and lines of code (LoC), are abundant and cheap to collect. But high numbers do not mean developers are working on the right things or solving meaningful problems. These metrics are also easy to game: Developers can increase commit frequency, split PRs artificially, or write more code without delivering more value. Higher activity does not equal better outcomes.</p>
  <p>– <b>Automated data is enough.</b> Log-based and telemetry metrics (e.g., deployments, build times, PR throughput) capture system behavior and process outputs. They are valuable for tracking trends continuously over time. But they rarely provide enough context to interpret what is actually happening or to infer suitable interventions. For that, self-reported (qualitative) signals are essential: Periodic surveys and targeted follow-up interviews surface the human experience behind the numbers, explaining why a metric looks the way it does and pointing toward interventions that automated data alone could never suggest.<sup>3</sup></p>
  <p>– <b>There is a perfect set of metrics for our company.</b> It is tempting to search for a definitive metric set: borrowing from a well-known engineering blog, adopting a popular framework without tailoring, or replicating what a high-profile company does. But as one senior engineering leader at a large financial company observed: “There is no universal set of metrics that will solve all the problems you face as a business. What works depends entirely on your tooling, your team structure, and the situation you find yourself in. It requires a very good understanding of how to develop metrics in the first place.” Metrics need to be tailored to your own context, goals, and hypotheses for improvement. Designing metrics that stakeholders will trust is itself demanding: Google’s productivity-research team frames a good metric as one that is intuitive to a broad audience, reflects patterns understood to exist in the world, doesn’t reflect patterns we know don’t exist, and is useful in decision-making.<sup>9</sup></p>
  <p>– <b>The numbers tell the whole story.</b> Even well-chosen metrics are highly context dependent. A team tracking deployment frequency after a platform migration may see numbers drop, not because delivery slowed, but because they consolidated services. Interpreting metrics well requires understanding the processes, infrastructure, and organizational context behind the numbers. That understanding is hard to build and easy to get wrong.</p>
  <p>&nbsp;</p>
  <h3>The DevEx Metrics Compass: Navigating 120 Metrics Across Research and Industry</h3>
  <h4>Building the Dataset</h4>
  <p>To understand how DevEx is actually measured across both research and industry, we conducted a structured curation effort. This consisted of reviewing scientific literature alongside public company resources: engineering blogs, conference talks, case studies, and podcasts from more than 50 organizations, including Google, Microsoft, Atlassian, Spotify, Capital One, and Stripe. The goal was not to create yet another framework but to map the landscape as it actually exists, capturing the broad range of metrics that researchers study and that practitioners report using.</p>
  <p>Each metric was annotated across multiple dimensions, chosen to reflect the practical decisions a team faces when selecting metrics: how data is collected (self-reported via surveys or interviews, automated via logs and telemetry, or both); collection maturity (what is achievable when getting started versus what requires established instrumentation or advanced custom tooling); the framework or company where it originated; whether it is relevant to measuring AI impact; and what outcome goal it serves (developer experience, product excellence, or organizational effectiveness).<sup>4</sup></p>
  <p>The result is a dataset of 120-plus metrics organized across eight dimensions: developer enablement, system, process, business, quality, team, output, and customer. These dimensions reflect the range of concerns that matter for DevEx: from the immediate conditions that impact daily work (e.g., build times, cognitive load, focus time) through the outputs of that work (e.g., throughput, produced code), to the downstream outcomes it produces (e.g., customer satisfaction, business value). Together they form the structure of the DevEx Metrics Compass.</p>
  <p>&nbsp;</p>
  <h4>The Compass: Three Steps from Exploration to Implementation</h4>
  <p>The DevEx Metrics Compass is a public interactive dashboard available at <a href="http://devexcompass.com/">devexcompass.com</a>. Rather than prescribing a fixed metric set, it is designed as a navigation and comparison tool—built on top of, and complementary to, existing frameworks such as DORA, SPACE, and DX Core 4 rather than replacing them. The Compass is organized as a three-step workflow: Explore the landscape, compare metric sets, then review and implement a shortlist.</p>
  <p>– <b>Step 1: Explore.</b> The main view presents all 120-plus metrics as a sunburst chart, with the eight dimensions forming the middle ring and individual metrics arranged in the outer segments (figure 1). A filter panel on the right allows users to tailor the view along any of the annotation dimensions: data-collection type, collection maturity, company size, outcome goal, research framework, specific company, and AI relevance. A popularity filter (e.g., 10 percent of the most-referenced metrics across sources) helps cut through the volume.</p>
  <p>Selecting any metric opens a detailed overlay showing a plain-language definition, synonymous names used across different organizations, the number of sources referencing it, which companies track it, and which research papers propose or validate it. At any point, users can mark a metric as “already tracking” or “plan to track,” building a personal shortlist throughout the session. For teams new to DevEx measurement, a guided wizard reduces the cold-start problem of facing 120-plus metrics at once: It asks a few questions about context, scope, and maturity, then preconfigures the filters accordingly.</p>
  <div style="border:1px solid black;padding:20px;margin:20px;">
   <p>Alex opens the Compass and marks five existing metrics as already tracking: deployment frequency, throughput, daily/weekly/monthly active users for AI tools, AI Spend, and percentage of AI-generated code—all automated, all pulled from continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD) and the AI tool dashboard. Mapped onto the sunburst chart, a pattern is immediately visible: Existing metrics fall into process, output, business, and customer dimensions. Developer enablement, team, quality, and system are entirely uncovered. A different picture emerges after filtering by “AI impact” and browsing what the 25 percent most-referenced sources are measuring.Alex adds several automated metrics to the plan-to-track list, each addressing a question the current set cannot answer: AI suggestion acceptance rate (Are developers trusting what the AI produces?), diff authoring time (Is AI shifting time from writing code to reviewing it?), code rework rate (Is AI-generated code holding up over time?), change failure rate (Is delivery stability holding or degrading?), and mean time to recover (When incidents happen, how quickly does the team recover?).</p>
