  <h4>Emerging Patterns Across 120-Plus Metrics</h4>
  <p>The DevEx Metrics Compass dataset consists of 120-plus metrics drawn from scientific literature and the public practices of more than 50 engineering organizations, offering a broad view of how DevEx is measured today. Several patterns stand out:</p>
  <p>– <b>Perceived satisfaction and DORA metrics are most referenced.</b> A small set of metrics shows broad consensus across both research and industry, while everything else fragments quickly. Developer satisfaction is the most broadly referenced metric overall, appearing in 45 sources. The (original four) DORA metrics follow closely: change failure rate (27 times), deployment frequency (21 times), cycle time (19 times), and MTTR (16 times)—reflecting broad consensus specifically around delivery-performance measurement. Two widely applied output-oriented measures are throughput (27 times) and produced code (25 times). A newer cluster of metrics is emerging around AI tool usage: AI tool adoption rate (23 times) and daily/weekly/monthly active users for AI tools (18 times), reflecting the growing demand to understand how developers engage with AI tooling. Beyond these widely shared metrics, coverage fragments quickly across eight dimensions with little agreement on what else matters, illuminating a field that is still, to a large degree, figuring out what to measure and why.</p>
  <p>– <b>The further you move from day-to-day engineering activity, the fewer established metrics exist.</b> The dimensions closest to the craft and conditions of software development—process (25 times), developer enablement (26 times), and quality (20 times)—are the most referenced. System (11 times) and output (9 times) follow, capturing operational outcomes such as system reliability, build stability, and developer output. Team (6 times), customer (7 times), and business (16 times), by contrast, reflect broader organizational and relational outcomes: how teams collaborate, how end users experience the software, and how engineering contributes to business value. The latter ones are the hardest to instrument and attribute causally, suggesting a broader tendency to focus on what developers produce over the conditions that make sustainable, high-quality work possible in the first place. This gap is particularly consequential, given that collaboration is also the SPACE dimension where developers report the least perceived impact from AI,<sup>11</sup> making it both the hardest to measure and to improve without better instrumentation.</p>
  <p>– <b>Research and industry overlap broadly, but diverge on well-being and AI.</b> A common assumption is that what research suggests and what industry actually measures differs. The data tells a more nuanced story: 63 percent of metrics in the dataset appear in both research and industry sources, suggesting stronger convergence between theory and practice than the fragmented framework landscape might imply. Yet sharp divergences exist at the edges. Well-being metrics such as cognitive load, focus time, and interruptions are well established in research but have very little industry adoption. These are the dimensions most relevant to sustainable developer productivity, yet they remain largely confined to academic study. At the other extreme, AI-specific metrics are almost entirely industry driven: metrics such as acceptance rate of AI suggestions, percentage of AI-generated code, and unused AI licenses have emerged rapidly in company measurement practices, with little to no research validation behind them (yet).</p>
  <p>– <b>Most teams are measuring what is easy, not what matters most.</b> In the dataset, 72 percent of metrics are collected via automated instrumentation, reflecting the natural pull toward what can be automated and collected continuously. But the dimensions most relevant to sustainable productivity (i.e., developer enablement and team) show the highest proportion of self-reported metrics. Collaboration quality, autonomy, learning and development, and cognitive load all require data collection through surveys or interviews. Organizations that rely exclusively on automated instrumentation are not getting a complete picture; they are systematically missing the signals that explain why their quantitative metrics look the way they do, why certain trends are emerging, and what to do about it.</p>
  <p>Note: These observations reflect the current state of the dataset. Many companies do not publicly share their measurement practices or provide only a partial picture, and research naturally lags behind fast-moving industry practices—particularly visible with AI metrics, given academic review cycles. The open-sourced dataset will continue to evolve as additional sources are identified and reported.</p>
  <p>&nbsp;</p>
  <h4>From First Steps to Systematic Comparison: Navigating DevEx Metrics at Any Maturity Level</h4>
  <p>The observations just mentioned raise an immediate practical question: Given this landscape, where should an organization start with DevEx measurement and improvement? The answer depends heavily on measurement maturity, including the instrumentation, resources, and goals currently in place. The Compass organizes metrics across three cumulative levels that reflect this reality, from teams taking their first steps to organizations with dedicated DevEx functions and custom tooling:</p>
  <p>– <b>Getting started.</b> This covers 59 metrics available out of the box from standard tooling (code repository, CI, issue tracker) or a short survey, without any custom instrumentation needed.</p>
  <p>– <b>Established.</b> This level builds on the first level, adding metrics that require combining data sources, a structured survey instrument, or light custom instrumentation, bringing the total to 105 metrics achievable within a few weeks of setup.</p>
  <p>– <b>Advanced.</b> This includes the full dataset, covering metrics that require significant custom tooling, IDE and/or calendar integration, dedicated research infrastructure, or ongoing qualitative data collection. These are typically the domain of organizations with a dedicated DevEx or research team.</p>
